                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Look at the Art", body: "Walk into any church in the world — Catholic, Orthodox, Protestant, doesn't matter. Look at the paintings. Look at the stained glass. Look at the icons. Look at the mosaics.\n\nWhat do you see behind the heads of Jesus, Mary, and every saint?\n\nA circle. A glowing disk. A ring of light.\n\nThey call it a HALO. Or a NIMBUS. Or a GLORY.\n\nBut look at it. Really look at it.\n\nIt's a SUN DISK.\n\nThe same golden circle that appears behind the heads of Egyptian sun gods, behind Hindu deities, behind Buddhist figures, behind Persian Mithras. The exact same visual element — a radiant circle representing solar divinity — placed behind Jesus's head in every piece of Christian art for two thousand years.\n\nThey never hid it. They painted it. They carved it. They sculpted it. They gilded it in gold. They put it on every wall of every church on every continent.\n\nAnd they never once told you what it actually represents.\n\nBecause if they said out loud that the golden disk behind Jesus's head is the same solar symbol that appeared behind Ra, behind Apollo, behind Sol Invictus, behind Mithras — the conversation about Christian origins would become very uncomfortable very fast."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "The Halo's Actual History", body: "The nimbus/halo predates Christianity by thousands of years.\n\nANCIENT EGYPT: Ra and other solar deities depicted with a sun disk above or behind the head. Pharaohs wore the uraeus (cobra) with the sun disk to show divine solar authority.\n\nANCIENT GREECE: Apollo (sun god) depicted with radiant light emanating from his head. Helios drove the sun chariot with a radiant crown.\n\nPERSIA / MITHRAISM: Mithras — the Persian solar deity whose cult was Christianity's direct competitor in Rome — always shown with a radiant halo or sun disk. Mithras was born December 25th, from a virgin, in a cave, witnessed by shepherds, had 12 companions, performed miracles, died, and rose after 3 days. Sound familiar?\n\nHINDUISM / BUDDHISM: Deities depicted with a prabhavali (luminous aura) or prabhaman-dala (circle of light) behind the head, representing divine radiance and spiritual illumination.\n\nEARLY CHRISTIANITY: For the first 300 years, Christians did NOT use halos. Jesus was depicted as a regular man — a young shepherd, a philosopher. The halo only appears in Christian art AFTER Constantine's conversion in the 4th century — precisely when Christianity absorbed Roman solar worship traditions.\n\nThe halo entered Christianity at the exact moment the Roman Empire merged its sun worship with the new state religion.\n\nCoincidence? Or blueprint?\n\nLook at early depictions of Christ in the catacombs (2nd-3rd century) — no halo. Now look at post-Constantine mosaics (4th-5th century) — halo everywhere. The transition is historically documented and visually obvious.\n\nThey didn't inherit the halo from Jesus. They inherited it from Sol Invictus — the Unconquered Sun — Rome's official solar deity that Constantine worshiped BEFORE his conversion and whose imagery he simply transferred onto Christ."),
