                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Why Churches Face East", body: "Here's something you can verify right now on Google Maps: the vast majority of churches in Europe and the Americas are built facing EAST.\n\nThe entrance is in the west. The altar is in the east. When the congregation faces the altar, they face the rising sun.\n\nThis is called liturgical orientation — and the word ORIENTATION itself comes from the Latin oriens, meaning \"east\" or \"rising.\" To ORIENT something literally means to point it toward the sunrise.\n\nWhy would a religion about a carpenter from Nazareth build every single house of worship pointing toward the sunrise?\n\nBecause the practice wasn't invented for Jesus. It was inherited from solar worship traditions that predated Christianity by millennia. Egyptian temples faced east. Greek temples of Apollo faced east. Mithraic temples (mithraea) were oriented to catch the sunrise on the solstice. The orientation IS the theology — and the theology is solar.\n\nTHE ROSE WINDOW\nThe massive circular stained-glass windows on the front of Gothic cathedrals — Notre Dame, Chartres, Westminster — are called ROSE WINDOWS.\n\nThey're circular. They radiate outward from a center point. They glow when sunlight hits them.\n\nThey're SUN WHEELS. The same geometric pattern used in solar worship across every ancient culture — a circle with radiating spokes. The very design converts sunlight into a display of color and glory inside the church.\n\nThe building is literally engineered to channel the sun's light through solar geometry onto the congregation.\n\nTHE MONSTRANCE\nIn Catholic tradition, the Eucharist (communion host) is displayed in a golden vessel called a MONSTRANCE. Look one up. Google it right now.\n\nIt's a golden SUN with radiating rays, and the communion wafer — a small white CIRCLE — sits at the center.\n\nThe congregation kneels before it. They worship the small white circle surrounded by golden sun rays.\n\nThey're worshiping the sun. With a wafer in the middle.\n\nAnd nobody says it."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "The Cross as Solar Symbol", body: "THE SOLAR CROSS\nThe cross predates Christianity by thousands of years. The simplest cross — a vertical and horizontal line intersecting — is one of humanity's oldest symbols, found in Neolithic cave art, Bronze Age petroglyphs, and ancient astronomical markers.\n\nThe equal-armed cross (like a plus sign) represents the four cardinal directions, the four seasons, the two solstices and two equinoxes — the sun's annual journey divided into four quarters.\n\nThe CELTIC CROSS — a cross with a circle behind it — is explicitly a solar cross. The circle is the sun. The arms are the four seasons. It's still used on Christian churches today throughout Ireland, Scotland, and Wales.\n\nLook at the back of every crucifix, every church steeple, every cathedral — many incorporate a circle behind the cross. The sun behind the intersection. Solar theology encoded in the most recognizable Christian symbol on earth.\n\nEVEN THE CRUCIFIX ITSELF\nThe Latin cross (with one arm longer than the others) also maps to solar movement. The vertical axis = the solstice axis (summer at top, winter at bottom). The horizontal axis = the equinox axis (spring and fall). The intersection = the center point, the sun.\n\nJesus on the cross = the sun at the center of the four seasons.\n\nEaster = spring equinox (resurrection after winter death).\nChristmas = winter solstice + 3 days (born again December 25th).\n\nThe LITURGICAL CALENDAR itself is a solar calendar with Jesus's life events mapped onto the sun's annual cycle. Birth at winter solstice. Death and resurrection at spring equinox. The church year IS the solar year, with different names.\n\nThey didn't map Jesus onto the calendar. They mapped the calendar onto Jesus. Or more precisely — they mapped the sun's story onto a historical figure and built an institution around the fusion."),
